---
/* THE IMPRINT. Section 5 DDG, and the parts of it that apply to this site.

   THE HEADING NAMES THE DDG AND NOT THE TMG. The Telemediengesetz ceased to
   apply on 2024-05-14 and the Digitale-Dienste-Gesetz took its place. A page
   headed "in accordance with section 5 TMG" cites a statute that no longer
   exists, which is the single most common defect on a German site and one that
   has been warned over. Naming the section is optional -- what is prescribed
   is the information, not the citation -- but where it is named it has to be
   the live one.

   IT IS IN ENGLISH ONLY, AND THAT FOLLOWS FROM WHO READS IT. Section 5 DDG
   requires the details to be easily recognisable, directly accessible and
   permanently available. Article 12 (1) GDPR requires the information it
   governs to be concise, transparent, intelligible and in clear, plain
   language. Both standards are addressed at the audience the service is aimed
   at, and neither prescribes a language; there is no supreme-court decision
   that supplies one either.

   This site is English throughout, its readers are international and
   English-speaking, and there is no sales relationship to the German market
   behind it. English is therefore the language in which its readers can
   actually understand these pages, which is what both provisions ask for.

   German law applies regardless: section 3 DDG puts a German provider under
   German law wherever the reader sits. That is a different question from the
   language the page is set in, and the two are easy to run together.

   The language question is not conclusively settled, and ADR 0258 carries it
   as a deliberately borne interpretation risk together with the conditions
   that reopen it.

   THE BLOCKS THAT ARE NOT HERE ARE NOT MISSING. Register court, register
   number, VAT identification number, supervisory authority for a licensed
   trade, and the MStV responsible party each apply to a case this provider is
   not in, and the template rule is that a block which does not apply is
   deleted rather than filled with a dash: an imprint with empty rubrics draws
   the eye to exactly the thing it does not have. ../lib/legal carries which
   and why, including the one most likely to change.

   THE LEGAL FORM IS NOT STATED, and it was for one afternoon. It was struck
   without replacement on the owner's instruction: what section 5 (1) no. 1 DDG
   asks for beside the name is the legal form of a legal person, and the two
   lines above already identify a natural person trading under a business name.
   Nothing was substituted for it, and nothing else in the address block moved
   with it.

   THE DATA PROTECTION AUTHORITY IS NOT HERE EITHER, and it is not an omission.
   It belongs to the right to complain under Article 77 GDPR, which is a
   privacy notice's subject; naming it here as well would be a second copy of a
   fact that has an owner one page over.

   WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE AT ALL: the liability and link disclaimers
   that circulate in every German boilerplate imprint. They rest on a judgment
   that never said what they claim it said, they change nothing about liability
   under sections 7 to 10 DDG, and their only reliable effect is to make an
   imprint read as copied. */
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { ENTITY } from '../lib/legal';
import { ORG, SITE } from '../lib/site';
---
<Legal
  title="Imprint"
  lede="Who publishes wordscript.dev, and how to reach them."
>
  <h2>Information required under section 5 DDG</h2>
  <address>
    {ENTITY.name}<br />
    {ENTITY.holder}<br />
    {ENTITY.street}<br />
    {ENTITY.postcode} {ENTITY.city}<br />
    {ENTITY.country}
  </address>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Email: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
  </p>
  {/* Rendered only when there is a number. ../lib/legal says why it is a null
      and not a placeholder, and why the deploy script refuses while it is. */}
  {ENTITY.phone && (
    <p>Telephone: <a href={`tel:${ENTITY.phone.replace(/\s/g, '')}`}>{ENTITY.phone}</a></p>
  )}

  <h2>The brand this site publishes under</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is built and published under {ORG.name}, the open source brand
    of {ORG.parent.name}. {ORG.name} is a brand rather than a separate legal
    entity: the provider named above is responsible for this site and for the
    software it describes.
  </p>

  <h2>Consumer dispute resolution</h2>
  <p>
    We are neither willing nor obliged to take part in dispute resolution
    proceedings before a consumer arbitration board.
  </p>
</Legal>
